  "h1": "Affordable Care Act (ACA) Marketplace Coverage vs Medicaid",
  "introText": "Medicaid and the ACA are not the same thing: the Affordable Care Act (ACA) is a law, Marketplace plans are private health insurance, and [Medicaid](https://www.healthcare.gov/medicaid-chip/getting-medicaid-chip/) is a joint federal and state public coverage program. In an ACA vs Medicaid comparison, Marketplace plans are bought through the [Health Insurance Marketplace](https://www.healthcare.gov/quick-guide/one-page-guide-to-the-marketplace/), while each state runs Medicaid within federal guidelines. [HealthCare.gov](https://www.healthcare.gov/medicaid-limited-benefits/) states that a person in a Medicaid program that counts as minimum essential coverage is not eligible for a premium tax credit or other savings on a Marketplace plan.\n\nLimited-benefit Medicaid works differently. A new 2027 Medicaid work requirement can also leave some adults with Marketplace access but no savings. The Marketplace or the state Medicaid agency makes the eligibility decision for each person in a household.",
  "tableHeading": "Marketplace Coverage and Medicaid Are Different",
